This ARM template snippet for a VM fails validation:

```json
{
  "type": "Microsoft.Compute/virtualMachines",
  "name": "vm1",
  "apiVersion": "2022-11-01",
  "location": "eastus2",
  "zones": ["1","2"],
  "properties": {
    "availabilitySet": {
      "id": "/subscriptions/.../resourceGroups/rg/providers/Microsoft.Compute/availabilitySets/as1"
    },
    "hardwareProfile": { "vmSize": "Standard_D2s_v5" },
    "networkProfile": { "networkInterfaces": [ { "id": "/.../nic1" } ] }
  }
}
```
The plan is to deploy two standalone VMs and pin each to a different zone (no scale sets). What is the minimal change to fix validation and meet the plan?

You run Terraform with the following files and environment:

```terraform
// variables.tf
variable "location" {
  type    = string
  default = "centralus"
}
```

```terraform
// main.tf
provider "azurerm" { features {} }

resource "azurerm_resource_group" "rg" {
  name     = "rg-demo"
  location = var.location
}
```

```terraform
// terraform.tfvars
location = "westeurope"
```

```text
Environment:
TF_VAR_location=eastus
```

No -var or -var-file flags are used on the CLI. When you `terraform apply`, which location is used for the resource group, and why?

You use an Azure Storage backend for Terraform state:

```terraform
terraform {
  backend "azurerm" {
    resource_group_name  = "rg-tfstate"
    storage_account_name = "tfstate123"
    container_name       = "tfstate"
    key                  = "prod.terraform.tfstate"
  }
}
```

Two engineers start `terraform apply` against the same workspace within seconds of each other. What happens to the second apply?

You deploy an App Service with a Key Vault reference in Bicep:

```text
resource kv 'Microsoft.KeyVault/vaults@2022-07-01' existing = {
  name: 'kv-demo'
}

resource web 'Microsoft.Web/sites@2022-03-01' = {
  name: 'app-demo'
  location: resourceGroup().location
  properties: {
    siteConfig: {
      appSettings: [
        {
          name: 'ConnString'
          value: '@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=${kv.properties.vaultUri}secrets/DbConn/abcdef...)'
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

No `identity` is configured on the web app. After a successful deployment, the first HTTP request to the app fails with a Key Vault reference resolution error. What change fixes this without hardcoding secrets into templates?

You assign the built-in Allowed Locations policy via Terraform:

```terraform
resource "azurerm_policy_assignment" "allowed_locs" {
  name                 = "enforce-locations"
  policy_definition_id = data.azurerm_policy_definition.allowed_locations.id
  scope                = data.azurerm_subscription.primary.id
  parameters = jsonencode({
    listOfAllowedRegions = {
      value = ["westeurope", "northeurope"]
    }
  })
}
```

The policy definition expects a parameter named `listOfAllowedLocations`. What is the outcome of `terraform apply`?

You add an explicit dependency so Terraform creates Key Vault access before deploying an App Service with a Key Vault reference:

```terraform
resource "azurerm_key_vault_access_policy" "app" {
  key_vault_id = azurerm_key_vault.kv.id
  tenant_id    = data.azurerm_client_config.current.tenant_id
  object_id    = azurerm_windows_web_app.app.identity[0].principal_id
  secret_permissions = ["Get", "List"]
}

resource "azurerm_windows_web_app" "app" {
  name                = "wv-demo"
  location            = azurerm_resource_group.rg.location
  resource_group_name = azurerm_resource_group.rg.name
  identity {
    type = "SystemAssigned"
  }
  app_settings = {
    "ConnString" = "@Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=${azurerm_key_vault.kv.vault_uri}secrets/DbConn/...)"
  }
  depends_on = [azurerm_key_vault_access_policy.app]
}
```

Despite `depends_on`, the first cold start after deployment intermittently fails with 403 from Key Vault. Why?
